\chapter{Overview}
\label{Overview}

FeedbackFruits (FBF) is an LTI~1.3 tool that runs inside Canvas.
When an FBF activity is graded, FBF sends Canvas one aggregate per student: a
score and, at best, a filled-in rubric.
The data behind that aggregate (who reviewed whom, the score on each
criterion, the feedback text, the comments) stays in FBF.
This package fetches that data.

There is no documented API and no login of our own: KTH users only reach FBF
through an LTI launch from Canvas.
The package therefore has three parts, which are also its main modules.
\begin{description}
\item[The launch] (\cref{Launch}) turns a Canvas API token into an FBF
  access token.
  It asks Canvas for a sessionless launch URL, which carries a one-time
  Canvas login, and then plays the browser's part in the LTI~1.3 handshake:
  two auto-submitting forms and a chain of redirects.
\item[The API client] (\cref{API}) uses the access token for FBF's own
  JSON:API, the one FBF's web app uses.
  One launch is enough for every FBF course the user teaches, since the token
  belongs to the user, not to the launched activity.
\item[The HAR tools] (\cref{HAR}) handle what makes this fragile: the API is
  private and changes without notice.
  They reduce a browser capture (a HAR file) to its \emph{shape}---the hops of
  the launch, the endpoints, the resource types with their attribute
  names---without a single value, so the shape can be committed and compared
  with a fresh capture when something breaks.
\end{description}

The launch is the fragile part with the most moving pieces, so it reports a
failure by naming the hop that broke.
The API client depends on the launch only through the access token, which
lets us test and replace the two independently.


